The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (so->tx.buf != so->tx.sbuf)
    kfree(so->tx.buf);
```
and
```c
// inside isotp_sendmsg(), after cmpxchg sets so->tx.state to ISOTP_SENDING
isotp_fill_dataframe(so, ...);  // reads so->tx.buf
```
Reason:
In `isotp_release()`, the `while` loop waits for `so->tx.state == ISOTP_IDLE`. If a signal interrupts the wait, the loop exits early, the state is forcefully set to `ISOTP_SHUTDOWN`, and then `kfree(so->tx.buf)` is executed. Meanwhile, `isotp_sendmsg()` uses `cmpxchg()` on `so->tx.state` to serialize access and may still be reading `so->tx.buf` in `isotp_fill_dataframe()` (e.g., for the final CAN frame). This results in a use-after-free of `so->tx.buf`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
- if (so->tx.buf != so->tx.sbuf)
-     kfree(so->tx.buf);
```
and
```c
+static void isotp_sock_destruct(struct sock *sk)
+{
+    struct isotp_sock *so = isotp_sk(sk);
+    can_sock_destruct(sk);
+    if (so->rx.buf != so->rx.sbuf)
+        kfree(so->rx.buf);
+    if (so->tx.buf != so->tx.sbuf)
+        kfree(so->tx.buf);
+}
```
and in `isotp_init()`:
```c
+    sk->sk_destruct = isotp_sock_destruct;
```
Reason:
The `kfree()` of extended buffers is moved from `isotp_release()` (which can race with `isotp_sendmsg()`) to `isotp_sock_destruct()`. This destructor is called via `sk_destruct` only when the last reference to the socket is released (after both `isotp_release()` and `isotp_sendmsg()` have completed). By deferring the `kfree()` to that point, the buggy race condition is eliminated because `so->tx.buf` is guaranteed to no longer be in use.

